{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R77nhHdM6CZ2LhsXindduX","type":"tool_result","content":"1464\t=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n1465\t\n1466\t---\n1467\t\n1468\t## Chapter 4 update (2026-08-24)\n1469\t\n1470\t| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n1471\t|---|---|---|---|\n1472\t| **D1.1** | Chapter 3 *(cluster layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n1473\t| **D1.1** | **Chapter 4** *(object layer)* | **deep** | **2026-08-24** |\n1474\t\n1475\t**Registry row change:** `D1.1 | Kubernetes Core Concepts | Ch 3, 4, 5, 6` →\n1476\tstatus becomes **\"in progress — Ch 3 and Ch 4 covered 2026-08-24\"**.\n1477\t\n1478\t### Chapter 4 — D1.1 coverage detail\n1479\t\n1480\t`kb_tags.objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`; all six sections carry `objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`. Authored\n1481\tweight estimate **~6%**, disclosed inline with the correct caveat: the chapter states the\n1482\tpublished figure (Kubernetes Fundamentals at 44%) and says plainly that CNCF publishes no\n1483\tweights for individual competencies inside a domain. **That is the compliant pattern — it\n1484\tnames the published number, marks the estimate as the book's, and points at the front\n1485\tmatter for the derivation. Copy it.**\n1486\t\n1487\t| Sub-topic | Depth |\n1488\t|---|---|\n1489\t| The object model; \"record of intent\"; the four required fields | deep |\n1490\t| `spec` vs `status`, and the authorship asymmetry | **deep — discharges Chapter 3's forward promise** |\n1491\t| Declarative vs imperative; the three management techniques | deep — **closes Chapter 1's reserved term** |\n1492\t| Object name vs UID | moderate — **plants Chapter 5's replacement rule** |\n1493\t| Namespaces; the four initial namespaces; no nesting | deep |\n1494\t| Namespaced vs cluster-scoped | **deep — B3 theme, originates here** |\n1495\t| ConfigMap: size, scope, four consumption paths, immutability | deep |\n1496\t| Secret: types, base64, default storage posture | deep |\n1497\t| Labels, selectors, `matchLabels` ≡ `matchExpressions`+`In` | **deep — B3 theme, originates here** |\n1498\t| Annotations, and the label/annotation rule | deep |\n1499\t| Pod as a primitive | **still deferred to Ch 5** (⚠ used ~80× here, undefined) |\n1500\t| PodSpec | **deferred to Ch 5 — confirmed by outcome; Ch 4 does not mention it** |\n1501\t| Secret hardening / encryption at rest / RBAC | **deliberately deferred to Ch 12** |\n1502\t| `kubectl` command surface | **deliberately deferred to Ch 8** |\n1503\t\n1504\t---\n1505\t\n1506\t## ⚑ Book-level fact to correct in the chapter\n1507\t\n1508\tChapter 4's Safe Harbor reads **\"Chapter 4 of 15 complete.\"** The book has **20 chapters**\n1509\t— `chapter-lineup.md` runs to row 20 (`Full Mock Exam`), with row 19 the synthesis chapter.\n1510\tVerified on disk 2026-08-24. Chapters 1 (L376) and 3 (L950) both reference Chapter 17 by\n1511\tname, so the figure is contradicted by shipped text as well as by the plan.\n1512\t\n1513\t---\n1514\t\n1515\t## ⚑ Ethical-guardrail status — Chapter 4\n1516\t\n1517\t| Chapter | Guardrail #8 (frequency claims) | Note |\n1518\t|---|---|---|\n1519\t| Ch 1 | pass | |\n1520\t| Ch 2 | pass | models the compliant phrasing |\n1521\t| Ch 3 | **FAIL — open** | six unverifiable exam-frequency assertions |\n1522\t| **Ch 4** | **BORDERLINE — five prevalence superlatives** | see below |\n1523\t\n1524\tChapter 4 avoids Chapter 3's specific failure mode — it makes **no** \"frequently tested\" or\n1525\t\"cheap points on exam day\" claims about exam behavior. What it does carry are five\n1526\t**practitioner-prevalence** superlatives stated as fact:\n1527\t\n1528\t- §4 — \"the single most common day-one surprise with ConfigMaps\"\n1529\t- §4 — \"This pair produces more confident wrong answers than anything else in this chapter\"\n1530\t- Safe Harbor — \"a piece of knowledge a surprising number of working practitioners have wrong\"\n1531\t- Exam Alert — \"the detail that gets asked\" · \"the one candidates miss\"\n1532\t\n1533\tThese sit in the register the brand licenses (experienced practitioner judgment) rather\n1534\tthan being fabricated statistics, and the underlying judgments are almost certainly right.\n1535\t**\"The single most common\" is the one that overreaches** — it is a superlative about\n1536\tpopulation frequency, stated flat. Author call; hedging it costs one word.\n1537\t\n1538\t**Separately, and outside guardrail #8:** §3 asserts twice that third-party preparation\n1539\tmaterial misstates `kube-public`. On the substance Chapter 4 is correct and the\n1540\tdocumentation backs it. But it is an unsourced claim about competitors' accuracy, and\n1541\tPart 14 forbids strawmanning alternative study methods. Adjacent rather than in breach —\n1542\tflagged for the author, not marked failing.\n1543\t\n1544\t**⚑ And the one that outranks both:** Chapter 4 **has no fact-accuracy audit at all.**\n1545\t`diagnostics/fact-accuracy.md` is a BLOCKER notice — Stage 6 received `[file not available]`\n1546\tfor both drafts and inspected zero claims. Guardrail #1 (\"no fabricated statistics or\n1547\tclaims\") **cannot be certified for this chapter.** Re-run Stage 6 against `draft-v2.md`\n1548\tbefore ship.\n1549\t=== END APPEND ===\n1550\t\n1551\t=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n1552\t\n1553\t---\n1554\t\n1555\t## Chapter 4 update (2026-08-24)\n1556\t\n1557\t**6 tagged items · graded pool 34 (13 Bearings + 21 Practice) · rate = 17.6%.**\n1558\tB3's rung for Chapter 4 is **15%. Cleared.** Chapter 4 is the first chapter to draw from\n1559\ttwo predecessors.\n1560\t\n1561\t| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n1562\t|---|---|---|\n1563\t| kube-apiserver receives the request; etcd stores the object | ch 3 §2, §5 | **ch 4** — Practice Q1 |\n1564\t| a controller acts on the difference between desired and current state | ch 3 §6 | **ch 4** — P